model {
  extend basalt {
    productionOps = module "Production operations" {
      #basalt #deep
      // An isolated environment that supplies the pool signing key must serve signed callbacks.
      // Its normal organization cleanup retains a pool so that dependency is exercised.
      description "The retired-token operator accepts only the current host storage version and refuses older or unknown schemas before reporting candidates. Refused signed operations write one server log record with an allowlisted guard and reason code preserved through phase wrappers. The public failure receipt stays unchanged; unknown errors expose only fixed phase and category codes, never raw messages or row values. A manual main-branch workflow signs fixed runtime operations with existing deploy credentials and an explicit organization. The API Worker serves tools gateway database operations with its existing database access and bindings; the MCP Worker refuses HTTP requests and loads no operator modules. A separately signed pool operation backs up exactly four reviewed Home credentials and their same-pool memberships, encrypting full rows before returning them to the workflow artifact store. Its saved dry run binds the deployed source, database and unchanged row checksums. Explicit GO uses Home credential DELETE and membership DELETE, never raw SQL writes. A started durable receipt stops uncertain retries, including fresh normal plans. Each removal finishes runtime readiness before its DELETE deadline and rechecks the reviewed snapshot before sending it. Failures retain a fixed phase, code, elapsed time, row identity and allowlisted downstream status without raw responses. A dedicated recovery plan binds the original incomplete receipt, saved plan and backup, exact identities and fresh row hashes. Recovery preserves the original receipt, atomically links separate progress, reconciles verified tombstones and requires a newly reviewed plan after interruption. Completion verifies tombstones and a retained credential checksum. Explicit duplicate-binding retirement accepts reviewed remove and retain IDs only when a verified replacement already covers every app selection in the same provider, method and placement. It preserves all retained rows and refuses active consumers, pending connections and shared legacy grants. New webhook registration dependencies lock their selected accounts in deterministic order and reread existence before committing the captured selection. Retirement and dependency creation therefore serialize on the same account rows. Affected history and source bookkeeping join the encrypted restore superset; retirement makes no provider revocation call and does not establish the removed grant subject. Retained outside-service identity repairs use read-only issuer-pinned proofs bound to saved grant and placement. Resource-bound identity repair also binds site, encrypted credential and app-link checksums; its SQL-only apply updates identity metadata while preserving both grants and every consumer. Every inventory is scoped to that organization. Affected-table backups remain an encrypted restore superset for a local recipient. Database checks bind the stable direct origin, a database-owned persistent UUID and the transaction-observed login role OID, adding the server identifier when its query succeeds; invocation-local proxy addresses, credentials and URL options never identify the target. The identity denotes dataset lineage; a byte-restored clone can retain it. Apply still rechecks every reviewed row, scope and stable key, and requires a plan younger than one hour. After restoring a database, the operator rotates its identity row before running any production-ops plan. Apply binds a successful saved dry-run, organization, exact GO, deployed source and unchanged row hashes; a serializable transaction lets SQL-only module authority locks precede the runner's scoped row locks, checks declared row ownership before and after writes, and records an organization-bound receipt. Blocked provider-account plans refuse before any request-selected lock; ready plans wait only for the organization advisory lock and existing member share. Every selected row is claimed with NOWAIT in deterministic order before scoped checks, so adoption cannot form a row-lock cycle with another writer. Contention returns structured SQL diagnostics and an explicit retryable busy reason, which the operator retains in failure.json. All operations refuse organization-removal tombstones with organization_removing and retryable false. Verification and replay recheck row scope. Failure classification examines typed failures, defects and all composite reasons; only fixed categories, cause kinds, phases, the stale_plan reason and allowlisted SQL state classes cross the runtime failure boundary. Stale plans require a fresh dry run. The CLI retains these fields plus the HTTP status in a separate safe failure receipt, and the workflow always uploads retained output after failure. Raw exception messages, response content and credentials stay out of failure output. The operator checks receipts before resolving an uncertain result. An exact-ID released-retirement repair requires a fresh signed read of the surviving Home reference and decrypted account-reference agreement before each first apply. Network reads hold no SQL lock. A serializable locked compare-and-swap rechecks complete connection, account and link fingerprints and removes only the exact released false marker; leases, successful removal, ambiguity or changed inputs refuse. Every other provenance field and credential remains untouched. Receipt replay only verifies the committed state. Local adoption proofs pause real SQL transactions through a loopback listener; product builds never load that proof gate."
      metadata { code "scripts/production-ops|scripts/production-ops.test.ts|.github/workflows/production-ops.yml"; libraries "node:http"; endpointHosts "127.0.0.1" }
    }
  }
}
